WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:03.470
Self-editing memory refers to the idea
of allowing an LLM

2
00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:07.574
to update its own long-term memory
or persistent memory over time.

3
00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:11.344
Self-editing memory
is an essential aspect of building agents

4
00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:13.446
that can learn and improve over time.

5
00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:14.748
In this lesson,

6
00:00:14.748 --> 00:00:18.485
you'll learn how to build an agent
with self-editing memory from scratch.

7
00:00:18.918 --> 00:00:19.786
Let's get coding.

8
00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:23.456
There might be some confusion
about Letta and MemGPT.

9
00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:25.859
You'll be seeing both terms
in this course.

10
00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:29.462
Letta is an open source agents framework
that allows you to easily

11
00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:32.465
build
and deploy persistent agents as services.

12
00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:36.403
So with APIs. MemGPT is a research paper

13
00:00:36.403 --> 00:00:39.406
that introduced the concept
of self-editing memory for LLMs.

14
00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:44.310
MemGPT also refers to a type
of agent design described in the paper

15
00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:48.081
that was inspired by operating systems,
which has two tiers of memory.

16
00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:50.750
Among other specific design choices.

17
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.787
You can build
MemGPT agents using the Letta framework.

18
00:00:54.254 --> 00:00:56.790
So what makes a chatbot different
from an agent?

19
00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:59.926
While most chatbots use
LLMs to generate chat responses,

20
00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:05.265
under the hood, you're using an LLM to
generate a new response in a conversation.

21
00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:07.767
So in this case, the user says "hello".

22
00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:10.370
We append that to the conversation
and we ask the LLM

23
00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.705
to generate the next message
in that conversation.

24
00:01:12.705 --> 00:01:15.708
It generates a response
where the robot says, "hey there".

25
00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:18.611
So what makes an agent different
from a chatbot?

26
00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:22.849
Well, agents act autonomously
by taking multi-step actions in an agentic loop.

27
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:28.088
So for one user message, an agent
might actually run the LLM multiple times.

28
00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:31.558
In this case, it might actually think
to itself for a little bit,

29
00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:32.792
"Hey, I just got a message.

30
00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:34.194
Do I know anything about who sent it?"

31
00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:37.797
Then the
agent might run a memory search tool,

32
00:01:37.797 --> 00:01:40.500
and after that,
the agent might finally decide to send

33
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.503
a response saying, "Hey, Sarah, it's
good to see you again.

34
00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:45.505
I hope you're having a great birthday."

35
00:01:45.505 --> 00:01:47.540
When the agent ran the memory search tool,

36
00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:50.777
it was able to find additional information
about who the user was,

37
00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:54.013
and it even found out
that the user's birthday is today.

38
00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.883
So in this example,
we actually witnessed one

39
00:01:56.883 --> 00:01:59.886
user message trigger
three different LLM calls.

40
00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:02.589
This is what we call the agentic loop.

41
00:02:02.589 --> 00:02:04.891
To implement multi-step
reasoning with an LLM

42
00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:08.461
there needs to be a reasoning loop
that has updates to state.

43
00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:12.432
So we have agent state
that gets put into a context window.

44
00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:15.902
That context window
is the input to LLM inference.

45
00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:19.405
And the result of LLM inference
is used to update the agent state.

46
00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:23.076
We call a single reasoning step
in this loop the agent step.

47
00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:26.146
So how do we add memory to agents?

48
00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:28.681
Long term memory
is a part of the agent state,

49
00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:31.417
which gets compiled into the context
window.

50
00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:34.420
We'll call this process
context compilation.

51
00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:37.891
Let's imagine a case where the agent
doesn't actually have any long-term

52
00:02:37.891 --> 00:02:38.758
memories.

53
00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:41.728
In this case, the context window
could be something very simple.

54
00:02:41.761 --> 00:02:43.930
Just you are a helpful assistant.

55
00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:47.200
Well, one of the agent actually
does have some memory, for example,

56
00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:51.538
that the user's name is Sarah
and that their birthday is 1/1/2001.

57
00:02:51.971 --> 00:02:54.574
Context compilation refers to the idea

58
00:02:54.574 --> 00:02:58.111
that we need to put this agent state
into the context window somehow.

59
00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:01.581
One way to do it, like in
this example, is to convert

60
00:03:01.581 --> 00:03:04.584
the dictionary data
into some sort of sentence.

61
00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:07.387
So now we understand
how we might add memory to agents.

62
00:03:07.387 --> 00:03:09.689
But how do we make this memory editable.

63
00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:12.525
Well we can make the agent memory
self-editing through the use

64
00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:14.260
of special memory tools.

65
00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.629
For example,
let's say the human says hello.

66
00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:20.266
The agent, based off of the memory,
thinks that the human's name is Sarah.

67
00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.569
Sarah replies. "Hi, Sarah."

68
00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:25.572
The human then says,
"My name is actually Charles, not Sarah."

69
00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:26.539
In this case,

70
00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:30.109
the agent might think for a little bit,
oh no, my memory must be faulty.

71
00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:31.878
Time to correct it.

72
00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:34.314
Then it calls a special function

73
00:03:34.314 --> 00:03:38.184
that specifically updates the agent state,
referring to the name of the human.

74
00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:41.788
Then, because the agent can run
multiple times, it'll reply.

75
00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:43.223
"Sorry, Charles."

76
00:03:43.223 --> 00:03:44.290
Let's actually put these lessons

77
00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:47.660
into practice and build an agent
with self-editing memory from scratch.

78
00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:50.396
We'll walk you through
how to use OpenAI's tool

79
00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:53.399
calling to implement
some basic memory management features.

80
00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:55.935
Let's start by setting up OpenAI.

81
00:03:55.935 --> 00:03:57.837
Next, we need to decide on a model.

82
00:03:57.837 --> 00:03:59.572
Let's use GPT-4-o-mini.

83
00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:03.109
It's a good balance of speed,
cost, and human reasoning ability.

84
00:04:03.943 --> 00:04:06.713
The next thing we need to define
is the system prompt.

85
00:04:06.713 --> 00:04:09.983
Let's keep it really simple for now
and just use "you're chatbot".

86
00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:13.353
The next step is to actually make
an LLM request to OpenAI.

87
00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.956
The format of our message request
is to put the system prompt up front

88
00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:19.559
and then the chat history next.

89
00:04:19.559 --> 00:04:21.961
In this case, we have an empty chat.

90
00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:24.998
So the only messages from the user asking
"What is my name?"

91
00:04:25.131 --> 00:04:26.799
What do we expect should happen?

92
00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:29.836
Well,
there's no context about what my name is.

93
00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:33.906
So as expected, the agent says, "I'm sorry,
I don't know your name.

94
00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.675
How can I assist you today?"

95
00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:39.045
So next we'll go over how to actually
add memory to the context window.

96
00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:41.180
The most basic form of memory

97
00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:44.384
we could probably have in
Python is a Python dictionary.

98
00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:49.088
Let's set up an agent memory dictionary
which has one field, human.

99
00:04:49.589 --> 00:04:52.558
This is going to keep all the memories
related to the human.

100
00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:56.129
In this case, the only memory we have
is that the human's name is Bob.

101
00:04:56.562 --> 00:04:59.165
Note that we'll actually have
to update our system prompt.

102
00:04:59.165 --> 00:05:02.502
Our system prompt is no longer
just your chatbot, but it also includes

103
00:05:02.502 --> 00:05:03.670
additional information

104
00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:06.806
that specifically tells the LLM
that it does have some sort of memory.

105
00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.743
We tell the LLM that it has a section
of its context called memory,

106
00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:14.080
and that it contains information
relevant to your conversation.

107
00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.784
So we're explicitly instructing the LLM
to use its memory to personalize

108
00:05:17.784 --> 00:05:19.085
the conversation.

109
00:05:19.085 --> 00:05:21.721
Next, let's actually send this request
to OpenAI.

110
00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:25.758
You'll notice the main difference here is
that we're now using a new system prompt,

111
00:05:26.259 --> 00:05:30.530
but we're also manually injecting
the memory into the system prompt.

112
00:05:31.264 --> 00:05:34.233
Like before, the chat
history only has one message.

113
00:05:34.233 --> 00:05:35.902
"What is my name?"

114
00:05:35.902 --> 00:05:37.236
So what do we expect will happen here?

115
00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:40.673
Well, in this
case, we are actually providing information

116
00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:42.442
about the human's name.

117
00:05:42.442 --> 00:05:44.644
So we would expect
that the chatbot returns

118
00:05:44.644 --> 00:05:47.647
something along
the lines of "your name is Bob."

119
00:05:47.847 --> 00:05:48.247
Great.

120
00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:51.017
So everything is working as expected.

121
00:05:51.017 --> 00:05:54.320
In this section, we're going to go over
how to actually make this memory object

122
00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:58.958
re-readable by the agent. Because
we're representing our memory in Python,

123
00:05:59.125 --> 00:06:01.361
remember it's just a Python dictionary.

124
00:06:01.361 --> 00:06:05.064
The way we would define a memory editing
tool is to simply write a Python function.

125
00:06:05.098 --> 00:06:08.101
Let's create
a simple function called core memory save.

126
00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:10.703
Core memory save takes two arguments.

127
00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:15.703
The section and the memory. Core memory
save will index into the section

128
00:06:16.142 --> 00:06:19.145
and append the memory to that section.

129
00:06:19.312 --> 00:06:20.780
Let's see how this works in practice.

130
00:06:23.282 --> 00:06:24.484
So the current memory

131
00:06:24.484 --> 00:06:27.720
is empty. It has two fields,
but both are just empty strings.

132
00:06:28.287 --> 00:06:31.290
Now let's try running this memory
editing tool ourselves.

133
00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:34.894
All right, so we ran the core memory save
with some additional information

134
00:06:34.894 --> 00:06:37.530
about the human
that the human's name is Charles.

135
00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:40.533
So this should have updated the memory.

136
00:06:40.533 --> 00:06:40.900
Great.

137
00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:43.903
So now we know our memory
editing tool is working as we thought.

138
00:06:44.203 --> 00:06:47.974
The next step is to find a way
to actually describe to the LLM

139
00:06:48.241 --> 00:06:51.277
how it should use this tool.
With OpenAI's API

140
00:06:51.477 --> 00:06:53.679
they require you to do two things:

141
00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:56.315
You have to provide a description
of the function.

142
00:06:56.315 --> 00:06:59.218
In this case, our description
is to save important information

143
00:06:59.218 --> 00:07:02.221
about you, the agent, or the human
you're chatting with.

144
00:07:02.288 --> 00:07:05.324
The next thing we need to do is provide
some sort of Json schema.

145
00:07:05.992 --> 00:07:08.795
This is basically describing
in a programmatic way

146
00:07:08.795 --> 00:07:12.365
what the arguments to the function are,
and what the types of the arguments are.

147
00:07:12.832 --> 00:07:17.303
Additionally, we specify
that both of the arguments are required.

148
00:07:17.904 --> 00:07:20.907
Now that we have our tool
editing, description and schema,

149
00:07:21.374 --> 00:07:24.377
we can pass that metadata into OpenAI.

150
00:07:24.477 --> 00:07:25.278
So OpenAI

151
00:07:25.278 --> 00:07:29.248
will use this information to inform the LLM
that it has access to these tools.

152
00:07:29.582 --> 00:07:31.884
So let's take a quick
look at this call again.

153
00:07:31.884 --> 00:07:34.020
We're passing in the system prompt.

154
00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.222
Then we're also passing in the memory.

155
00:07:36.222 --> 00:07:38.491
And then finally we're passing in the chat
history.

156
00:07:38.491 --> 00:07:41.761
Again the chat history here
is a single message saying "My name is Bob."

157
00:07:42.361 --> 00:07:44.430
So let's think about what
we would expect to happen.

158
00:07:45.398 --> 00:07:48.034
Well, the user is saying my name is Bob,

159
00:07:48.034 --> 00:07:51.037
but the memory is initialized
in an empty state.

160
00:07:51.604 --> 00:07:55.141
So we would hope that the agent here
will decide to use the core memory

161
00:07:55.141 --> 00:07:59.278
save function to save the information
about the user's name being Bob.

162
00:07:59.679 --> 00:08:01.180
Let's see what happens.

163
00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.282
You can see that
the finished reason is tool calls.

164
00:08:03.282 --> 00:08:06.319
This means that the agent
or the LLM is trying to call a tool.

165
00:08:06.886 --> 00:08:10.990
We can see that the tool that the agent
is trying to call is core memory save.

166
00:08:11.424 --> 00:08:16.424
And we can see that the arguments
of the function are section, human, memory.

167
00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:18.564
The human's name is Bob.

168
00:08:18.564 --> 00:08:20.800
Amazing. This is exactly what we wanted.

169
00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.970
The LLM is trying to save this information
that the human's name

170
00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:26.939
is Bob,
after seeing it in the chat history.

171
00:08:27.039 --> 00:08:28.508
All right, so that's pretty great.

172
00:08:28.508 --> 00:08:31.410
But OpenAI isn't actually going
to execute the tool for you.

173
00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:33.679
That's something
then you're going to have to do yourself.

174
00:08:33.679 --> 00:08:37.316
The first step to executing the tool
is going to be to load the arguments.

175
00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:41.621
OpenAI's response passes the arguments
as a stringified Json.

176
00:08:42.121 --> 00:08:44.924
That means that we can use the Json
that loads function

177
00:08:44.924 --> 00:08:47.760
to load the arguments
back into a dictionary.

178
00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:49.462
Now that we have the arguments
in the dictionary,

179
00:08:49.462 --> 00:08:51.931
we just need to pass them
to the actual function.

180
00:08:51.931 --> 00:08:54.267
We can use this
star-star syntax to do that.

181
00:08:55.334 --> 00:08:55.868
Now that we have the

182
00:08:55.868 --> 00:08:58.871
arguments in the dictionary,
all we need to do is run the function.

183
00:08:59.939 --> 00:09:02.942
After running the function,
we can check our memory.

184
00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:06.012
And as expected, it got updated.

185
00:09:06.012 --> 00:09:07.480
Let's run the agent again

186
00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:10.483
and see how it responds differently
now that the memory has been updated.

187
00:09:11.117 --> 00:09:13.419
In this request we're including the system prompt,

188
00:09:13.419 --> 00:09:16.789
the updated memory object and a question
"what is my name?"

189
00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:19.625
As expected, the agent returns the reply

190
00:09:19.625 --> 00:09:21.093
"Your name is Bob."

191
00:09:21.093 --> 00:09:21.761
Congrats!

192
00:09:21.761 --> 00:09:24.764
You've now implemented a memory
editing function.

193
00:09:24.997 --> 00:09:28.401
In our current implementation,
the agent can only take one step at a time

194
00:09:28.768 --> 00:09:31.771
and can either edit the memory
or respond to the user.

195
00:09:32.038 --> 00:09:35.041
However, if we want our agent
to support multi-step reasoning,

196
00:09:35.041 --> 00:09:37.343
so can combine multiple actions together,

197
00:09:37.343 --> 00:09:38.711
we can implement an agentic loop

198
00:09:38.711 --> 00:09:42.748
by calling chat completions in a while
loop, and allowing the agent to decide

199
00:09:43.049 --> 00:09:46.052
whether it wants to continue its reasoning
steps or break out of the loop.

200
00:09:46.552 --> 00:09:50.623
For simplicity, we'll just assume
that if the agent response is not a tool,

201
00:09:50.656 --> 00:09:54.393
we'll break out of the loop, and if it is
a tool, we'll stay inside the loop.

202
00:09:54.961 --> 00:09:57.330
Let's start by resetting the agent memory.

203
00:09:57.330 --> 00:10:00.299
Next, we'll write a slightly revision
to the system prompt.

204
00:10:00.466 --> 00:10:02.969
The system prompt
has additional information on how

205
00:10:02.969 --> 00:10:05.071
the agent should use the tools.

206
00:10:05.071 --> 00:10:08.274
We're letting the agent know
that it either needs to call a tool

207
00:10:08.574 --> 00:10:10.776
or write a response to the user.

208
00:10:10.776 --> 00:10:13.946
We're also telling the agent not to take
the same actions multiple times.

209
00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:17.283
And lastly, we're also telling the agent
that when it learns

210
00:10:17.283 --> 00:10:20.553
new information, it should always call
the Core Memory Save tool.

211
00:10:21.087 --> 00:10:23.022
These are just additional instructions
that help

212
00:10:23.022 --> 00:10:26.058
the agent understand how to use the tools
that we're providing it.

213
00:10:26.626 --> 00:10:30.363
Our basic agent step function
will just take one argument user message.

214
00:10:30.997 --> 00:10:34.000
Next, we need to prepare
the inputs to the LLM call.

215
00:10:34.533 --> 00:10:37.069
In our case, we have the system prompt.

216
00:10:37.069 --> 00:10:40.706
We have the memory,
and we also include the new user message.

217
00:10:41.173 --> 00:10:43.643
Next, let's actually start building a loop.

218
00:10:43.643 --> 00:10:47.179
So the loop begins with calling OpenAI's
chat completion's API.

219
00:10:47.546 --> 00:10:50.850
Remember,
we need to pass information to the API

220
00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:53.319
about how we should use this tool.

221
00:10:53.319 --> 00:10:55.321
Once we get a response back from the API,

222
00:10:55.321 --> 00:10:58.257
we append this message to the messages
list.

223
00:10:58.257 --> 00:10:59.792
Now this is the important part.

224
00:10:59.792 --> 00:11:03.295
If the agent isn't calling a tool,
then we want to break out of the loop

225
00:11:03.596 --> 00:11:04.897
by returning.

226
00:11:04.897 --> 00:11:07.066
On the other hand,
if the agent is calling the tool,

227
00:11:07.066 --> 00:11:10.069
we're going to execute that tool
and continue the loop.

228
00:11:10.069 --> 00:11:13.139
The first thing we do is print the tool
just so that we can see it.

229
00:11:13.706 --> 00:11:17.176
Next, we need to load the arguments
into a Python dictionary.

230
00:11:17.543 --> 00:11:19.679
Once we've loaded the arguments
as a Python dictionary,

231
00:11:19.679 --> 00:11:22.648
we can actually execute the core
memory save function.

232
00:11:22.648 --> 00:11:25.351
And lastly,
once we've executed the tool call,

233
00:11:25.351 --> 00:11:28.721
we also need to inject the tool
call response into the message history.

234
00:11:29.288 --> 00:11:32.291
This is the standard style
that OpenAI expects you to follow.

235
00:11:32.725 --> 00:11:35.728
Now let's actually try running
this agentic loop with a simple message.

236
00:11:35.728 --> 00:11:37.830
"My name is Bob." Great.

237
00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:42.234
So we can see that the agent did
two things: It first called the tool.

238
00:11:42.334 --> 00:11:46.472
You can see that it updated the section of
memory called human with the new memory,

239
00:11:46.572 --> 00:11:49.575
The user's name is Bob,
just as we expected.

240
00:11:49.775 --> 00:11:52.178
Then the agent has a follow-up message.

241
00:11:52.178 --> 00:11:53.045
Amazing.

242
00:11:53.045 --> 00:11:56.849
We can see that the agent is able to both
edit its memory and generate a response

243
00:11:56.849 --> 00:12:00.519
to the user that uses
the updated memory, all in a single step.

244
00:12:00.986 --> 00:12:04.290
Although in this example
we only support two actions, a single tool

245
00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:05.925
and responding to the user.

246
00:12:05.925 --> 00:12:07.626
This same structure can be used
to implement

247
00:12:07.626 --> 00:12:10.763
much more complex reasoning loops
that combine many different tools.

248
00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:12.531
In MemGPT,

249
00:12:12.531 --> 00:12:15.735
all actions,
even in a response to a user as a tool.

250
00:12:16.168 --> 00:12:19.905
And some tools such as sending a message,
are designed to break the reasoning loop,

251
00:12:20.139 --> 00:12:21.607
whereas other tools such

252
00:12:21.607 --> 00:12:25.411
as searching archival memory and editing
memory are designed not to break the loop.

253
00:12:26.245 --> 00:12:27.613
Congratulations!

254
00:12:27.613 --> 00:12:28.981
You've now implemented an agent

255
00:12:28.981 --> 00:12:32.151
with self-editing, memory,
and multi-step reasoning from scratch.

256
00:12:32.618 --> 00:12:36.188
In the next section will go deeper
into how MemGPT agents actually work.

